{"step_index":1138,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-10T14:23:46Z","thinking":"**Identifying the Root Cause**\n\nThe Tomcat server received a \"400 Bad Request\" due to a duplicate `Host` header. The Nginx server sent this duplicated header. The problematic headers include both `Host: map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr` and `Host: map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443`.\n\n\n**Diagnosing Further Issues**\n\nDuplicate `Host` headers were identified as originating from an Nginx configuration where both a configuration include and an explicit header setting existed, resulting in additive behavior. This was confirmed to be the root cause of the \"400\" error by inspection. A cross-origin request from port 443 to 9443 necessitates CORS headers, which were missing and causing client-side rejection of AJAX requests. Also, Hairpin NAT configurations must be considered for access within the internal network.\n\n**Implementing and Validating Fix**\n\nThe configuration was cleaned by removing the duplicate header, and full CORS headers and an `OPTIONS` handler were added. Syntax and reload were verified to be successful. End-to-end verification, including backend access, confirmed the issue was resolved, resulting in a successful \"200\" response with the expected headers.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"replace_file_content","args":{"AllowMultiple":"false","Description":"\"Update report with resolution of 400 Bad Request and CORS headers\"","EndLine":"74","Instruction":"\"Update the report with the root cause analysis, fix, and verification results\"","ReplacementContent":"\"---\\n\\n## 3. Root Cause Analysis of the Previous Failure\\n\\nWhen clicking **\\\"Test connection to server\\\"**, two distinct mechanisms were blocking the chain:\\n\\n### A. The Nginx Duplicate Host Header (Tomcat 400 Bad Request)\\n* **Discovery**: Tomcat (Spring Boot Actuator) complies strictly with **RFC 7230 / RFC 9112 Section 5.4**:\\n  > *\\\"A server MUST respond with a 400 (Bad Request) status code to any request message that contains more than one Host header field.\\\"*\\n* **Nginx Behavior**: In Nginx, specifying multiple `proxy_set_header` directives with the same header name in the same block does **not** override; it sends **multiple duplicate headers**.\\n* **The Culprit**: `03-map...conf` included `nginxconfig.io/proxy.conf` (which set `proxy_set_header Host $host;`) AND also explicitly set `proxy_set_header Host $http_host;`.\\n* **The Result**: Nginx was forwarding:\\n  ```http\\n  Host: map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr\\n  Host: map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr:9443\\n  ```\\n  Tomcat immediately aborted the connection with `400 Bad Request`.\\n\\n### B. Missing CORS Headers (Browser AJAX Block)\\n* In [`listOptions.js`](file:///usr/share/centreon/www/modules/centreon-map4-web-client/views/options/listOptions.js#L266):\\n  ```javascript\\n  function testServerAddressNg() {\\n    const urlServerNg = jQuery('input[name=\\\"server_address_ng\\\"]').val();\\n    jQuery.ajax({\\n      type : 'GET',\\n      url : `${urlServerNg}/centreon-map/api/latest/actuator/health`\\n    });\\n  }\\n  ```\\n* The test button is triggered directly by the **client web browser** from origin `https://extranetclient...` (port 443) targeting port 9443.\\n* Modern browsers strictly block cross-origin requests unless the target server returns `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Remediation Applied on `th2-revproxyp-fe1`\\n\\nThe configuration [`/etc/nginx/sites-available/03-map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr.conf`](file:///etc/nginx/sites-available/03-map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr.conf) has been upgraded:\\n\\n1. **Clean Head\n<truncated 3317 bytes>","StartLine":"50","TargetContent":"\"## 3. End-to-End Verification Results\\n\\n```mermaid\\nflowchart LR\\n    Central[\\\"th2-centprop-be1\\\\n(172.30.198.49)\\\\nCentreon Central PHP\\\"] -->|\\\"HTTPS :9443\\\\n(Strict TLS Verified)\\\"| RevProxy[\\\"th2-revproxyp-fe1\\\\n(172.30.198.33)\\\\nNginx Proxy\\\"]\\n    ExtUser[\\\"External Browser\\\\n(map.extranetclient...)\\\"] -->|\\\"HTTPS :9443\\\\n(Valid Public Cert)\\\"| RevProxy\\n    RevProxy -->|\\\"HTTPS :9443 (TLS)\\\\nproxy_ssl_verify off\\\"| Map[\\\"th2-centprop-map\\\\n(172.30.198.50)\\\\nTomcat Engine\\\"]\\n```\\n\\n| Source / Target | Test Command | Result | Status |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :---: |\\n| **Local Machine $\\\\rightarrow$ Port 9443** | `openssl s_client -connect 172.30.198.33:9443` | `CN=map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr`, Issuer `Let's Encrypt (YE2)` | **VERIFIED** |\\n| **Local Machine $\\\\rightarrow$ Port 9443** | `curl -sS -I https://map.extranetclient...:9443/` | `HTTP/2 301` $\\\\rightarrow$ `location: .../centreon-map/` (Strict TLS OK, no `-k`) | **VERIFIED** |\\n| **Central Backend (`.49`) $\\\\rightarrow$ Reverse Proxy** | `curl -sS -I https://map.extranetclient...:9443/` | `HTTP/2 301` (Strict TLS OK, no `-k`, `/etc/hosts` routing OK) | **VERIFIED** |\\n| **Central Backend (`.49`) $\\\\rightarrow$ Centreon Map** | `curl -sS -I https://map.extranetclient...:9443/centreon-map/` | `HTTP/2 400/401` (Centreon Map application response reached) | **VERIFIED** |\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 4. Final Verification in the Centreon Web UI\\n\\nYou can now return to the Centreon Web Interface:\\n1. Navigate to: **`Administration > Extensions > Options`**.\\n2. Under the Map server settings, click **\\\"Test connection to server\\\"**.\\n3. Because the certificate is now natively trusted by PHP cURL with an exact SAN match for `map.extranetclient.outremer-telecom.fr`, the test will succeed with a green status.\\n\"","TargetFile":"\"/home/koby/.gemini/antigravity-cli/brain/6237460b-4163-4167-a26e-b8194f657433/centreon-map-chain-report.md\"","toolAction":"\"Updating report\"","toolSummary":"\"Update centreon-map-chain-report.md\""}}],"truncated_fields":["tool_calls"]}
